 <div class="map"><h2>4. What this room is for and how it works</h2><p>The room's capability map: its purpose, every part and what it does, what flows in from and out to other rooms, and what it must never become.</p><div class="wrap">
-  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Getting to Signed · Live Instrument · capability map</div><h1>The deal isn’t won when they say yes.</h1><div class="fam">Live Instrument (live execution) · renamed from Negotiation 2026-07-07 · settled (face-off + ledger)</div><p>Get a <b>verbally-agreed</b> deal actually <b>signed</b> — walk the seller through every front between “we want to buy” and a countersigned contract, push back where they should push back, and keep the buying committee engaged so the deal doesn’t die in procurement. The most common way a B2B deal dies is not losing the argument — it’s <b>internal alignment collapse</b> after the verbal yes.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.16b (the run-to-signed reconceive + rename, 2026-07-07) + B2B procurement research (SIG/CAIQ · SOC 2/ISO shortcut · liability caps · MAP). Settled design: getting-to-signed-faceoff-2026-07-07.html.</div></div>
-  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">A <b>war room for the run to signed</b>, not a rehearsal booth. It leads with the one thing actually blocking right now (the biggest gap between their ask and your line), shown as a <b>face-off</b> — their side ⟷ the gap ⟷ your line — with the seller’s counter, a trade they can offer, and the line they won’t cross. Everything else open sits below as a positions ledger.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The four disciplines</div><h2>What the room enforces</h2><table><tr><th style="width:26px">#</th><th style="width:230px">Discipline</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td><span class="n">1</span></td><td><b>Every concession is a deliberate move</b></td><td>a give always asks for something back — never a reflex to “be reasonable”</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">2</span></td><td><b>You already hold the answers</b></td><td>a security questionnaire isn’t 150 blank lines — the coverage map shows how many your papers (SOC 2 · ISO 27001 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA) already answer</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">3</span></td><td><b>Keep the committee warm</b></td><td>a papers-ready strip + a plan-to-signed so nobody goes quiet</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">4</span></td><td><b>Terms are linked, not a list</b></td><td>a liability cap is worthless-or-fine depending on the indemnification ask — linked terms are settled together</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The fronts</div><h2>One ledger row each; the hottest is the face-off</h2><div class="sd">The room morphs which front is the face-off by what’s actually blocking, not a fixed order.</div><table><tr><th style="width:150px">Front</th><th>What it covers</th></tr><tr><td><b>Legal</b></td><td>liability cap + super-cap + indemnification, redlines</td></tr><tr><td><b>Security / InfoSec</b></td><td>the questionnaire + the coverage map + what still needs engineering</td></tr><tr><td><b>Finance</b></td><td>payment terms, prepay/discount trades, the signer</td></tr><tr><td><b>Business</b></td><td>keep the committee moving — champion + economic-buyer engagement, the plan-to-signed</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The deal</b></td><td>From Deal Workspace — value, stage, days-to-target.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The committee</b></td><td>champion / economic buyer / new stakeholders, each with an engagement read (warm / quiet N days / new).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The papers-ready strip</b></td><td>SOC 2 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The face-off</b></td><td>their side · the gap · your line + hold/trade/line + “send the counter-position” + “loop in an advisor who’s won this fight.”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The positions ledger</b></td><td>per-front row: their ask → your line → status (blocking / clearing / to-do / at-risk); each row drills to its depth in place.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The coverage map (security)</b></td><td>a bar of answered-by-your-papers vs still-open, with which paper answers how many, and how many open ones need engineering.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The payment-terms trade set</b></td><td>net-60 flat · net-30 for a cut · % off for annual up-front.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The plan-to-signed</b></td><td>the mutual dated run to signature, co-owned with the champion.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The “we won’t repeat this” log</b></td><td>feeds loss patterns.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="sd">Held for the <b>Pro gate</b>: RFP-response handling (formal RFP/RFI intake, question-splitting, drafted answers) — real and wanted, but scoped to a later version, not the first-ship room.</div><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b> (value, stage, decision architecture)</li><li>the pilot’s evidence from <b>Pilot Desk</b> (leverage in a terms conversation)</li><li>an advisor from <b>Call in a Favor</b> (a backchannel warming a front)</li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>front outcomes + the plan-to-signed → <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>loss-pattern feedback → <b>Future Autopsy</b> (lost in procurement, before terms?)</li><li>a stuck front → <b>Call in a Favor</b> (route a backchannel to the frozen front)</li><li>patterns → <b>Founding GTM</b> §6 (why we win) + §4 (where deals leak)</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Language — plain, no procurement jargon</div><h2>The teardown</h2><div class="sd">The seller is a first-timer facing their first enterprise legal/security gauntlet. Security frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001, SIG, DPA, pen-test) are named plainly because they’re the real objects — but each is framed as a paper the seller holds (“answers 88 of the questions”), never an acronym to decode.</div><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>“counterparty”</td><td>their side / their legal / their security team</td></tr><tr><td>“concession ladder”</td><td>what you can trade</td></tr><tr><td>“redline” (unless taught)</td><td>the change they want to the contract</td></tr><tr><td>the economic buyer</td><td>the person who signs off</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">7 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The run-to-signed mind (the deal isn’t won at yes)</li><li>The face-off on the blocking front (their side ⟷ your line, always the one actually blocking)</li><li>Every-concession-is-a-trade</li><li>You already hold the answers (the coverage map, never a 150-line data-entry chore)</li><li>Keeping the committee warm (the plan-to-signed, the engagement reads)</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not a pricing calculator</li><li>Not a rehearsal booth</li><li>Never soften into “negotiation tips”</li><li>RFP tooling is held for the Pro gate — not in the base room</li></ul></div></div></div>
+<div class="callout blue"><div class="ct">Language and capability review · 2026-11-02</div><div class="cb">Use the current source and docs/language-remediation-2026 for implementation. Earlier design references below preserve history; they do not authorize deprecated customer language or capabilities that the source does not implement. Keep stable internal identifiers separate from display wording. Distinguish evidence, assessment, forecast, and recommendation.</div></div>
+  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Getting to Signed · execution workflow · capability map</div><h1>Manage the approval and signature process.</h1><div class="fam">execution workflow (live execution) · renamed from Negotiation 2026-07-07 · settled (position comparison + ledger)</div><p>Getting to Signed organizes legal, security, finance, and business approval workstreams. Show the customer request, proposed position, gap, owner, and next action.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.16b (the run-to-signed reconceive + rename, 2026-07-07) + B2B procurement research (SIG/CAIQ · SOC 2/ISO shortcut · liability caps · MAP). Settled design: getting-to-signed-faceoff-2026-07-07.html.</div></div>
+  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Prioritize the issue currently blocking approval. Preserve the comparison of customer and seller positions, linked terms, document coverage, and dated mutual plan. Do not make unsupported claims about why deals are generally lost.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The four disciplines</div><h2>Operating principles</h2><table><tr><th style="width:26px">#</th><th style="width:230px">Discipline</th><th>What it means</th></tr><tr><td><span class="n">1</span></td><td><b>Every concession has a stated purpose</b></td><td>a give always asks for something back — never a reflex to “be reasonable”</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">2</span></td><td><b>You already hold the answers</b></td><td>a security questionnaire isn’t 150 blank lines — the coverage map shows how many your papers (SOC 2 · ISO 27001 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA) already answer</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">3</span></td><td><b>Keep stakeholders informed</b></td><td>a papers-ready strip + a plan-to-signed so nobody goes quiet</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">4</span></td><td><b>Terms are linked, not a list</b></td><td>a liability cap is worthless-or-fine depending on the indemnification ask — linked terms are settled together</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The workstreams</div><h2>One ledger row each; the hottest is the current blocking issue</h2><div class="sd">Select the current blocking workstream from recorded status, rather than a fixed category order.</div><table><tr><th style="width:150px">Workstream</th><th>What it covers</th></tr><tr><td><b>Legal</b></td><td>liability cap + super-cap + indemnification, redlines</td></tr><tr><td><b>Security / InfoSec</b></td><td>the questionnaire + the coverage map + what still needs engineering</td></tr><tr><td><b>Finance</b></td><td>payment terms, prepay/discount trades, the signer</td></tr><tr><td><b>Business</b></td><td>keep the committee moving — champion + economic-buyer engagement, the plan-to-signed</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The deal</b></td><td>From Deal Workspace — value, stage, days-to-target.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The committee</b></td><td>champion / economic buyer / new stakeholders, each with an engagement read (warm / quiet N days / new).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The papers-ready strip</b></td><td>SOC 2 · subprocessor list · pen-test · DPA.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The current blocking issue</b></td><td>their side · the gap · your proposed position + hold/trade/line + “send the counter-position” + “loop in an advisor who’s won this fight.”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The positions ledger</b></td><td>per-workstream row: their ask → your proposed position → status (blocking / clearing / to-do / at-risk); each row drills to its depth in place.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The coverage map (security)</b></td><td>a bar of answered-by-your-papers vs still-open, with which paper answers how many, and how many open ones need engineering.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The payment-terms trade set</b></td><td>net-60 flat · net-30 for a cut · % off for annual up-workstream.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The plan-to-signed</b></td><td>the mutual dated signature process, co-owned with the champion.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The “we won’t repeat this” log</b></td><td>feeds loss patterns.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="sd">Held for the <b>Pro gate</b>: RFP-response handling (formal RFP/RFI intake, question-splitting, drafted answers) — real and wanted, but scoped to a later version, not the first-ship room.</div><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b> (value, stage, decision architecture)</li><li>the pilot’s evidence from <b>Pilot Desk</b> (leverage in a terms conversation)</li><li>an advisor from <b>Call in a Favor</b> (a backchannel warming a workstream)</li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>workstream outcomes + the plan-to-signed → <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>loss-pattern feedback → <b>Future Autopsy</b> (lost in procurement, before terms?)</li><li>a stuck workstream → <b>Call in a Favor</b> (route a backchannel to the frozen workstream)</li><li>patterns → <b>Founding GTM</b> §6 (why we win) + §4 (where deals leak)</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Language — plain, no procurement jargon</div><h2>The teardown</h2><div class="sd">The seller is a first-timer facing their first enterprise legal/security gauntlet. Security frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001, SIG, DPA, pen-test) are named plainly because they’re the real objects — but each is framed as a paper the seller holds (“answers 88 of the questions”), never an acronym to decode.</div><table data-copy-context="deprecated-example"><tr><th style="width:230px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>“counterparty”</td><td>their side / their legal / their security team</td></tr><tr><td>“concession ladder”</td><td>what you can trade</td></tr><tr><td>“redline” (unless taught)</td><td>the change they want to the contract</td></tr><tr><td>the economic buyer</td><td>the person who signs off</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">7 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The signature-process mind (the deal isn’t won at yes)</li><li>The current blocking issue on the blocking workstream (their side ⟷ your proposed position, always the one actually blocking)</li><li>Every-concession-is-a-trade</li><li>You already hold the answers (the coverage map, never a 150-line data-entry chore)</li><li>Stakeholder communication, recorded engagement, and a dated signature plan</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not a pricing calculator</li><li>Not a rehearsal booth</li><li>Never soften into “negotiation tips”</li><li>RFP tooling is held for the Pro gate — not in the base room</li></ul></div></div></div>
