 <div class="map"><h2>4. What this room is for and how it works</h2><p>The room's capability map: its purpose, every part and what it does, what flows in from and out to other rooms, and what it must never become.</p><div class="wrap">
-  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Pilot Desk · Decision Bench · capability map</div><h1>A hand-holding guide to a pilot that actually converts.</h1><div class="fam">Decision Bench (strategic shaping) · renamed from PoC Framework 2026-07-06 · settled (the guided pilot)</div><p>A hand-holding pilot guide that walks a first-timer through running a pilot that converts — from setup, through getting the right people using it, to a result the buyer’s boss can act on without you in the room. <b>Not a scorecard; a guide.</b> A pilot is a people operation, not a metric — the success meter is adoption, not whether the outcome number moved yet.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.15 (the guided-pilot mind expansion + proof/foundry eradication, 2026-07-06) + shipped src/poc-framework. Settled design: pilot-desk-guided-2026-07-06.html.</div></div>
-  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Guide a first-timer through a pilot that gets your hooks in — pull the hands-on users (usually <b>not</b> the buyer or champion), discover hidden stakeholders, multi-thread deeper, and find problems they didn’t know they had. The room is a <b>slow unfolding</b> — one movement at a time, never a wall of fields. It <b>feels</b> customizable; the desk shapes around the deal.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The five movements</div><h2>Shown one at a time</h2><table><tr><th style="width:26px">#</th><th style="width:210px">Movement</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><span class="n">1</span></td><td><b>Set it up</b></td><td>what it has to show · the window · who signs off</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">2</span></td><td><b>Get the right people in</b></td><td>the hands-on users + champion + who signs off — <b>right-sized</b> (a tight sale needs a small group; the desk sizes the target to the deal)</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">3</span></td><td><b>Keep the pilot moving</b></td><td>hold their hand between check-ins (no rigid weekly clock) — is the product getting used, who else can you pull in</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">4</span></td><td><b>Read the adoption</b></td><td>are enough of the right people using the product day-to-day</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">5</span></td><td><b>Write up what it showed</b></td><td>a short write-up the champion can take to their boss and defend on their own</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The linked deal</b></td><td>The deal the pilot is attached to (from Deal Workspace).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The pilot spec</b></td><td>what it has to show · today’s number · how you’ll measure it · who signs off · when you’ll stop.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The window</b></td><td>Variable, not a fixed 7/14.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The circle</b></td><td>A right-sized headcount of who’s hands-on, sized to the deal — headcount lives only here.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Who’s-missing prompts</b></td><td>“Someone from IT” / “a day-to-day driver” with an inline find-them → name-them + ask-the-champion-for-an-intro flow.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The adoption meter</b></td><td><i>actually using it</i> (of the people you enrolled, how many are active) + <i>actions taken</i> — fed from the product’s own usage or marked by hand.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Gated per-check-in steps</b></td><td>A step unlocks only when the prior is done.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The Share kit</b></td><td>A collapsible library to drive adoption + arm the champion: getting-started video · per-role quick-starts · case study · “why you’re in this pilot” note · champion kit · kickoff announcement · FAQ · reference-customer intro.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The Mutual pilot plan</b></td><td>You + the champion agree the goal, steps, and results meeting — co-owned, a shared action, not a solo template.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The write-up</b></td><td>The short document the champion carries to their boss.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>the deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b></li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>pilot state + risk into <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>the stop rule into <b>Future Autopsy</b></li><li>portable evidence into <b>Call in a Favor</b></li><li>pilot state into <b>Handoff</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Language — the plain-words eradication</div><h2>No foundry, no “proof”</h2><div class="sd">The old room was a foundry metaphor drenched in banned words — all eradicated on the face. Code enum keys may stay; no user ever sees the banned word.</div><table><tr><th style="width:260px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>proof · cast · mold · forge/foundry · ingot · heat/heat-ledger · kill rule · readout</td><td>the pilot · the results · the evidence</td></tr><tr><td>the quality band</td><td>Ready / Almost / Too thin to hand over</td></tr><tr><td>the mold grid</td><td>the parts of the pilot</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The guide (never a scorecard or checklist)</li><li>Adoption as the success meter (never the outcome number alone)</li><li>The people / multi-threading layer (right-sized, never “get everyone”)</li><li>The who’s-missing prompt</li><li>The feel-of-ease (never a wall of fields)</li><li>The Share kit + Mutual pilot plan</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Never a scorecard</li><li>Never a checklist</li><li>Never a wall of fields</li><li>Not a metrics dashboard</li></ul></div></div></div>
+<div class="callout blue"><div class="ct">Language and capability review · 2026-11-02</div><div class="cb">Use the current source and docs/language-remediation-2026 for implementation. Earlier design references below preserve history; they do not authorize deprecated customer language or capabilities that the source does not implement. Keep stable internal identifiers separate from display wording. Distinguish evidence, assessment, forecast, and recommendation.</div></div>
+  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Pilot Desk · decision-support workflow · capability map</div><h1>Plan a pilot and evaluate its results.</h1><div class="fam">decision-support workflow (strategic shaping) · renamed from PoC Framework 2026-07-06 · settled (the guided pilot)</div><p>Pilot Desk records participants, stakeholder roles, success measures, evidence, and the next decision for a linked deal. Preserve its staged workflow and customization.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.15 (the guided-pilot mind expansion + proof/foundry eradication, 2026-07-06) + shipped src/poc-framework. Settled design: pilot-desk-guided-2026-07-06.html.</div></div>
+  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Help the seller define what the pilot must establish, who evaluates the result, and when the next decision occurs. Measure adoption and results without treating activity alone as buying intent.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · The five movements</div><h2>Shown one at a time</h2><table><tr><th style="width:26px">#</th><th style="width:210px">Movement</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><span class="n">1</span></td><td><b>Set it up</b></td><td>what it has to show · the window · who signs off</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">2</span></td><td><b>Get the right people in</b></td><td>the hands-on users + champion + who signs off — <b>right-sized</b> (a tight sale needs a small group; the desk sizes the target to the deal)</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">3</span></td><td><b>Keep the pilot moving</b></td><td>hold their hand between check-ins (no rigid weekly clock) — is the product getting used, who else can you pull in</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">4</span></td><td><b>Review adoption</b></td><td>are enough of the right people using the product day-to-day</td></tr><tr><td><span class="n">5</span></td><td><b>Write up what it showed</b></td><td>a short write-up the champion can take to their boss and defend on their own</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · What it offers</div><h2>The primitives</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The linked deal</b></td><td>The deal the pilot is attached to (from Deal Workspace).</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The pilot spec</b></td><td>what it has to show · today’s number · how you’ll measure it · who signs off · when you’ll stop.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The window</b></td><td>Variable, not a fixed 7/14.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The circle</b></td><td>A right-sized headcount of who’s hands-on, sized to the deal — headcount lives only here.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Who’s-missing prompts</b></td><td>“Someone from IT” / “a day-to-day driver” with an inline find-them → name-them + ask-the-champion-for-an-intro flow.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The adoption meter</b></td><td><i>actually using it</i> (of the people you enrolled, how many are active) + <i>actions taken</i> — fed from the product’s own usage or marked by hand.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Gated per-check-in steps</b></td><td>A step unlocks only when the prior is done.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The Share kit</b></td><td>A collapsible library to drive adoption + arm the champion: getting-started video · per-role quick-starts · case study · “why you’re in this pilot” note · champion kit · kickoff announcement · FAQ · reference-customer intro.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The Mutual pilot plan</b></td><td>You + the champion agree the goal, steps, and results meeting — co-owned, a shared action, not a solo template.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The write-up</b></td><td>The short document the champion carries to their boss.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li>the deal from <b>Deal Workspace</b></li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>pilot state + risk into <b>Deal Workspace</b></li><li>the stop rule into <b>Future Autopsy</b></li><li>portable evidence into <b>Call in a Favor</b></li><li>pilot state into <b>Handoff</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Language — the plain-words eradication</div><h2>No foundry, no “proof”</h2><div class="sd">The old room was a foundry metaphor drenched in banned words — all eradicated on the face. Code enum keys may stay; no user ever sees the banned word.</div><table data-copy-context="deprecated-example"><tr><th style="width:260px">Banned on the face</th><th>Say instead</th></tr><tr><td>proof · cast · mold · forge/foundry · ingot · heat/heat-ledger · kill rule · readout</td><td>the pilot · the results · the evidence</td></tr><tr><td>the quality band</td><td>Ready / Almost / Too thin to hand over</td></tr><tr><td>the mold grid</td><td>the parts of the pilot</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">6 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>The guide (never a scorecard or checklist)</li><li>Adoption as the success meter (never the outcome number alone)</li><li>The people / multi-threading layer (right-sized, never “get everyone”)</li><li>The who’s-missing prompt</li><li>The feel-of-ease (never a wall of fields)</li><li>The Share kit + Mutual pilot plan</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Never a scorecard</li><li>Never a checklist</li><li>Never a wall of fields</li><li>Not a metrics dashboard</li></ul></div></div></div>
   <div class="foot">REFERENCE · Pilot Desk capability map · compiled from canon §4.15 + shipped src/poc-framework + reconciled to current doctrine (2026-07-07). Settled design: pilot-desk-guided-2026-07-06.html</div>
