 <div class="map"><h2>4. What this room is for and how it works</h2><p>The room's capability map: its purpose, every part and what it does, what flows in from and out to other rooms, and what it must never become.</p><div class="wrap">
-  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Welcome · Threshold · capability map</div><h1>The first morning the setup pays back.</h1><div class="fam">Threshold (entry) · the seeding flow’s landing · design settled 2026-07-07</div><p>The threshold the operator hits on entry — the moment the app “rises up to greet” them and hands back the one move. Welcome is where the seeding flow <b>lands</b>: after setup the workspace is already deep (ICP + accounts + ≥10 deals + quota), so this is never an empty on-ramp — it is the first morning the hard work reads itself back.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.1 + the heavy-seeding doctrine (§12) + the seeding flow (§4.3). Settled design: welcome-flow-landing-2026-07-07.html.</div></div>
-  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">Close the loop the onboarding doorway opened — <b>“do the hard work once, the system reads your motion back to you every morning”</b> — and make that playback the whole room. One commanding statement, one dominant move (the system’s top pick, read overnight across the entire workspace), and the payback shown as a quiet secondary read. Progress is felt as <b>becoming operational</b>, never gamified, no finish line.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · What it offers</div><h2>Everything the room does</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The commanding statement</b></td><td>One serif line that names the state — day-one landing (“the workspace is awake”) vs re-entry (“what moved since you left”). The only thing that changes between the two lifecycles.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The one dominant move</b></td><td>The system’s single overnight pick + its plain reason + one CTA. The system read the whole workspace while the operator was away; this is the one thing that needs them first.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The “what the system saw” read</b></td><td>2–4 plain lines — the dividend on the setup work. Never a checklist, never a status dump. What the system noticed on its own.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The operating line</b></td><td>A recessive read: accounts watched · live deals · in flight. The “morning door” into the workspace — never “all done.”</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The calm route to the Dashboard</b></td><td>A quiet, non-competing path to the full command surface for the operator who wants more than the one move.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The two lifecycles</div><h2>One layout, two headlines</h2><div class="sd">Day-one landing and re-entry reuse the <b>exact same shape</b>; only the headline changes. This is the whole trick of the room — the payback surface is identical whether it’s the first morning or the hundredth.</div><table><tr><th style="width:150px">Lifecycle</th><th>Headline register</th><th>What the read says</th></tr><tr><td><b>Day-one landing</b></td><td>“The workspace is awake — here’s what you just switched on.”</td><td>the first move + what the seeding produced</td></tr><tr><td><b>Re-entry</b></td><td>“What moved since you left.”</td><td>account/deal movement since last session + the one move</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li><b>gtmos_activation_context</b> + the full seeded workspace from Onboarding (§4.3)</li><li>the system’s ranked pick from the <b>Dashboard</b> command-intelligence engine</li><li>account + deal movement since the last session</li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>directional handoff into the room that owns the one move — <b>Signal Console</b> / <b>Deal Workspace</b> / <b>Outbound</b></li><li>the calm route to the full <b>Dashboard</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>Welcome as the seeding flow’s <b>landing</b> — never an empty-workspace “create your first…” threshold</li><li>one dominant move (never two competing)</li><li>the payback made visible (“what the system saw while you were away”)</li><li>never gamified, no finish line</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not an empty on-ramp or a setup wizard</li><li>Not a dashboard of vanity counts</li><li>Not a gamified progress ladder</li><li>Not a “all done” celebration screen</li></ul></div></div></div>
+<div class="callout blue"><div class="ct">Language and capability review · 2026-11-02</div><div class="cb">Use the current source and docs/language-remediation-2026 for implementation. Earlier design references below preserve history; they do not authorize deprecated customer language or capabilities that the source does not implement. Keep stable internal identifiers separate from display wording. Distinguish evidence, assessment, forecast, and recommendation.</div></div>
+  <div class="mast"><div class="k">Welcome · Threshold · capability map</div><h1>Workspace setup and the next recommended action.</h1><div class="fam">Threshold (entry) · the onboarding data collection’s landing · design settled 2026-07-07</div><p>Welcome summarizes the configured workspace, the highest-priority recommended action, and recent changes. The recommendation and its evidence come from the Dashboard ranking engine.</p><div class="src">Sourced from: canon §4.1 + the heavy-seeding doctrine (§12) + the seeding flow (§4.3). Settled design: welcome-flow-landing-2026-07-07.html.</div></div>
+  <div class="sec"><div class="sn">1 · Purpose</div><h2>What the room is for</h2><div class="sd">After onboarding and on later visits, show the current setup state, the recommended action with supporting evidence, and account/deal totals. Keep one primary action and a secondary route to Dashboard. Do not claim that analysis happened overnight unless that execution is recorded.</div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">2 · What it offers</div><h2>Everything the room does</h2><table><tr><th style="width:230px">Capability / surface</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The setup-state heading</b></td><td>A prominent heading names setup completion or welcomes a returning user. Supporting information reflects the available account and deal evidence.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The highest-priority recommended action</b></td><td>The highest-ranked current recommendation, its evidence, and one action link. Do not imply an overnight execution that was not recorded.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>Recent changes</b></td><td>Two to four concise lines describing recent account and deal changes, with their supporting inputs.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The operating line</b></td><td>A secondary summary of watched accounts, active deals, and open pipeline.</td></tr><tr><td style="width:230px"><b>The calm route to the Dashboard</b></td><td>A quiet, non-competing path to the full command surface for the operator who wants more than the recommended action.</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">3 · The two lifecycles</div><h2>One layout, two headlines</h2><div class="sd">After setup and re-entry reuse the <b>exact same shape</b>; only the headline changes. This is the whole trick of the room — the payback surface is identical whether it’s the first morning or the hundredth.</div><table><tr><th style="width:150px">Lifecycle</th><th>Headline register</th><th>What the overview says</th></tr><tr><td><b>After setup</b></td><td>“Workspace setup is complete.”</td><td>the recommended action and available setup data</td></tr><tr><td><b>Re-entry</b></td><td>“Changes since your last visit.”</td><td>account/deal movement since last session + the recommended action</td></tr></table></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">4 · Flows</div><h2>What comes in, what goes out</h2><div class="flows"><div class="flowcol inn"><div class="fh">Flows in</div><ul class="plain"><li><b>gtmos_activation_context</b> + the full seeded workspace from Onboarding (§4.3)</li><li>the ranked recommendation from the <b>Dashboard</b> command-intelligence engine</li><li>account + deal movement since the last session</li></ul></div><div class="flowcol out"><div class="fh">Flows out</div><ul class="plain"><li>directional handoff into the room that owns the recommended action — <b>Signal Console</b> / <b>Deal Workspace</b> / <b>Outbound</b></li><li>the calm route to the full <b>Dashboard</b></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="sec"><div class="sn">5 · Guardrails</div><h2>Must never be flattened · what it is NOT</h2><div class="two"><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--red)">Must never be flattened</div><ul class="plain"><li>Welcome as the onboarding data collection’s <b>landing</b> — never an empty-workspace “create your first…” threshold</li><li>one primary recommended action (never two competing)</li><li>the payback made visible (“changes since your last visit”)</li><li>never gamified, no finish line</li></ul></div><div><div class="sn" style="color:var(--ink-3)">What it is NOT</div><ul class="plain"><li>Not an empty on-ramp or a setup wizard</li><li>Not a dashboard of vanity counts</li><li>Not a gamified progress ladder</li><li>Not a “all done” celebration screen</li></ul></div></div></div>
